<span class="ltx_personname">Andrey Y. Lokhov
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Theoretical Division T-5, Los Alamos National Laboratory
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study a discrete denoising diffusion framework that integrates a sample-efficient estimator of single-site conditionals with round-robin noising and denoising dynamics for generative modeling over discrete state spaces. Rather than approximating a discrete analog of a score function, our formulation treats single-site conditional probabilities as the fundamental objects that parameterize the reverse diffusion process. We employ a sample-efficient method known as Neural Interaction Screening Estimator (NeurISE) to estimate these conditionals in the diffusion dynamics.
Controlled experiments on synthetic Ising models, MNIST, and scientific data sets produced by a D-Wave quantum annealer, synthetic Potts model and one-dimensional quantum systems demonstrate the proposed approach. On the binary data sets, these experiments demonstrate that the proposed approach outperforms popular existing methods including ratio-based approaches, achieving improved performance in total variation, cross-correlations, and kernel density estimation metrics.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Machine Learning, ICML
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generative modeling over discrete spaces is fundamental to a wide range of applications, including molecular design, language modeling, and policy learning in reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Ermon, 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al., 2003</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. In these settings, data consist of categorical or binary variables with complex statistical dependencies, and accurately capturing their joint structure requires models that can scale to high-dimensional combinatorial configuration spaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While diffusion models have revolutionized generative modeling in continuous domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, their direct application to discrete data has received increased attention. Continuous-time formulations rely on Gaussian noise and score estimation through gradients of log-densities, quantities that are not well defined in discrete spaces. Naive relaxations, such as adding continuous noise to one-hot encodings, break the discrete structure and often yield poor sample quality or unstable training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These limitations motivate the need for a principled framework for <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">discrete diffusion processes</em> that preserves the combinatorial structure of the data, allows tractable inference, and retains the interpretability and scalability that made diffusion models successful in continuous domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A number of works have considered extending diffusion models to discrete spaces. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>)</cite> considers denoising diffusion for discrete data and discrete time (both absorbing and uniform diffusion), optimizing the variational lower bound (VLB) of the log-likelihood.
A continuous-time framework for discrete diffusion models is introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which also optimizes the VLB of the log-likelihood. The work
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2022</a>)</cite> performs score matching for continuous-time diffusion by learning conditionals using cross-entropy.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, the authors propose an approach to learn the discrete version of the score using a score-entropy function to ensure non-negativity of the score along the training iterations. For a broader survey on discrete diffusion models see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This paper’s contribution begins with making explicit that for forward transitions the canonical time-reversed kernel can be parameterized entirely through ratios of probabilities between configurations that differ at one coordinate, and that these ratios reduce exactly to ratios of single-site conditional distributions given the remaining coordinates. So reverse diffusion can be implemented by learning local conditionals instead of a global density or discrete <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">score.</span>
The main methodological contribution following this observation is to plug in
state-of-the-art estimator for learning discrete conditionals. We use the Neural Interaction Screening Estimator (NeurISE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jayakumar et al., 2020</a>)</cite> due to its approximation- and sample-efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A key conceptual observation is that, under the round-robin noising scheme of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Varma et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, autoregressive generation emerges as the hard-noise limit of the reverse-time sampler: each reverse step becomes “resample one coordinate from its single-site conditional,” and unrolling these updates in the fixed order yields autoregressive sampling (without proposing a new AR model). In contrast, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> also links absorbing diffusion to autoregressive generartion. Specifically, they connect absorbing diffusions any-order autoregressive models (AO-ARM) at the objective level through a reparameterization of the concrete score and a change of variables to masking probability. They show that in the infinite-noise limit the absorbing-diffusion loss equals the AO-ARM training objective. Thus, our bridge is a more direct, finite-step collapse of the sampler in a specific round-robin construction, whereas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> derives a broader equivalence through an algebraic transformation and limit of the training loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">On the theory side, the paper provides total-variation error-propagation bounds for data generation with an approximate reverse kernel, quantifying precisely how local inaccuracies accumulate across sampling steps, in the same vein as score-based sampling analyses for the continuous space or continuous time setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Ying, 2024</a>)</cite>. Conceptually, the bound isolates the two factors that drive sampling difficulty in diffusion models: (i) learning error in the learned reverse-time transitions, and (ii) how well the forward noising process mixes to the target noise distribution. This stands in contrast to Langevin or Glauber dynamics-type MCMC samplers, where convergence rates typically depend more directly on properties of the data distribution such as multi-modality, rather than on reverse-process estimation accuracy and forward-process mixing to noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we evaluate the proposed estimation approach for ratios of conditionals using NeuRISE on a range of discrete generative modeling
benchmarks, including synthetic Ising on a <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25</annotation></semantics></math>-variable system, binarized
MNIST, and <span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">quantum annealing (D-Wave) datasets</span>. Across these settings, we compare
against representative ELBO- and score-based methods: D3PM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and the SEDD method proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and demonstrate consistent improvements in distributional accuracy. A key experimental contribution is that the <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25</annotation></semantics></math>-variable Ising model provides a controlled setting to study how different denoising algorithms behave statistically as the training sample size varies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Problem</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> denote a discrete set of alphabets with cardinality <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Sigma|=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Sigma|=p</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the configuration space <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> discrete variables or coordinates, with elements denoted as <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:=(\sigma_{1},...\sigma_{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:=(\sigma_{1},...\sigma_{q})</annotation></semantics></math>. Given training samples from a probability distribution <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu:\Sigma^{q}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu:\Sigma^{q}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the goal is to construct a diffusion-based generative model from which new samples can be tractably generated</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We will specifically focus on a general class of probabilistic models over <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> defined using a Hamiltonian <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:\Sigma^{q}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:\Sigma^{q}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\sigma)=\frac{1}{Z}\exp\left(H(\sigma)\right),\quad\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>Z</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\sigma)=\frac{1}{Z}\exp\left(H(\sigma)\right),\quad\sigma\in\Sigma^{q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the partition function <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}}\exp\left(H(\sigma)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}}\exp\left(H(\sigma)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> ensures normalization. This formulation defines an energy-based probability distribution from the exponential family, where the energy function <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> typically encodes interactions between components of <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, such as pairwise terms or external fields.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An important example of such a model is the <em id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Ising model</em>, where <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma:=\{-1,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma:=\{-1,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>which is a pairwise Markov random field over <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> with interactions defined by an undirected graph <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=[q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=[q]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subseteq V\times V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subseteq V\times V</annotation></semantics></math>. The Hamiltonian takes the form:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\sigma)=\sum_{(i,j)\in E}J_{ij}\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}+\sum_{i\in[q]}h_{i}\sigma_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\sigma)=\sum_{(i,j)\in E}J_{ij}\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}+\sum_{i\in[q]}h_{i}\sigma_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{ij}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{ij}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the strength of the interaction between nodes <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> represents an external bias or field at node <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[q]</annotation></semantics></math>.
This model defines a probability distribution:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\sigma)=\frac{1}{Z}\exp\left(\sum_{(i,j)\in E}J_{ij}\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}+\sum_{i\in[q]}h_{i}\sigma_{i}\right),\quad\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>Z</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\sigma)=\frac{1}{Z}\exp\left(\sum_{(i,j)\in E}J_{ij}\sigma_{i}\sigma_{j}+\sum_{i\in[q]}h_{i}\sigma_{i}\right),\quad\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Equation (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2 Problem ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) defines the energy landscape that governs the distribution. The graph structure <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the conditional dependencies in the model, making the Ising model a special case of an undirected graphical model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals </h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a denoising diffusion framework over the configuration space <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, which consists of a known forward Markov process and a learned reverse process. The goal is to construct a forward Markov process <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{n}\}_{n=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{n}\}_{n=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\sim\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\sim\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the data distribution that we are interested in learning. The forward process then makes sure that the distribution <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to a tractable noise distribution, from which it is easy to sample: <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\mu_{n}=\mu_{\text{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>noise</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\mu_{n}=\mu_{\text{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Markov chain over <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> evolves according to a known transition kernel
<math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}:\Sigma^{q}\times\Sigma^{q}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}:\Sigma^{q}\times\Sigma^{q}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> which defines the conditionals <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma}):=\mathbb{P}(X_{n+1}=\sigma|X_{n}=\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma}):=\mathbb{P}(X_{n+1}=\sigma|X_{n}=\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The forward evolution of the distribution <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of the process <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is then given by:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n+1}(\sigma)=\sum_{\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n+1}(\sigma)=\sum_{\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Reverse Process</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To sample from the target distribution <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a reverse process <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Y_{n}\}_{n=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Y_{n}\}_{n=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{n}\sim\mu_{T-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{n}\sim\mu_{T-n}</annotation></semantics></math>. This reverse process is governed by a family of time-inhomogeneous transition kernels <math id="S3.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}^{\text{rev}}:\Sigma^{q}\times\Sigma^{q}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}^{\text{rev}}:\Sigma^{q}\times\Sigma^{q}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0},</annotation></semantics></math>
which satisfy the backward recurrence:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n}(\sigma)=\sum_{\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}k_{n}^{\text{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})\mu_{n+1}(\tilde{\sigma})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n}(\sigma)=\sum_{\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}k_{n}^{\text{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})\mu_{n+1}(\tilde{\sigma}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We obtain a natural candidate for the reverse-time transition kernel via Bayes’ rule is the following:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{n}^{\text{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{n}^{\text{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=k_{n}(\tilde{\sigma},\sigma)\cdot\frac{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}k_{n}(\tilde{\sigma},\hat{\sigma})\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=k_{n}(\tilde{\sigma},\sigma)\cdot\frac{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}k_{n}(\tilde{\sigma},\hat{\sigma})\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{k_{n}(\tilde{\sigma},\sigma)}{\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}k_{n}(\tilde{\sigma},\hat{\sigma})\cdot\frac{\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{k_{n}(\tilde{\sigma},\sigma)}{\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}k_{n}(\tilde{\sigma},\hat{\sigma})\cdot\frac{\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This expression shows that the reverse kernel depends only on the forward transition
probabilities and on ratios of the form
<math id="S3.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})/\mu_{n}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})/\mu_{n}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, if these ratios can be accurately
estimated we can construct accurate approximations to the reverse
kernels, <math id="S3.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can then sample from the noise distribution
<math id="S3.SSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math> and iteratively apply the reverse transitions to generate new samples
from an approximation of the data distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This captures the core idea of denoising diffusion probabilistic models (DDPMs). The
forward process gradually drives the data distribution toward a simple noise
distribution, while the reverse process reconstructs samples by inverting this dynamics
using learned conditional structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem formalizes this intuition. It provides an analogue of the convergence guarantees established for
score-based diffusion models in the continuous setting
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">De Bortoli et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Ying, 2024</a>)</cite>. The key insight is that the
discrepancy between the output distribution of the approximate reverse chain and the true
data distribution decomposes cleanly into two contributions: (i) the extent to which the
forward process has mixed toward the noise distribution, and (ii) the cumulative error
incurred when approximating the reverse kernels. This decomposition makes precise the
tradeoff underlying DDPM-style generative modeling. Accurate sampling requires both
sufficiently fast diffusion of the forward process to the noise distribution and sufficiently accurate estimation of the reverse-time
dynamics. Here, the total variation (TV) distance <math id="S3.SSx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{\rm TV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{\rm TV}</annotation></semantics></math> between two distributions is defined by <math id="S3.SSx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\hat{\mu}-\mu\|_{\mathrm{TV}}=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}}\bigl|\hat{\mu}(\sigma)-\mu(\sigma)\bigr|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\hat{\mu}-\mu\|_{\mathrm{TV}}=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}}\bigl|\hat{\mu}(\sigma)-\mu(\sigma)\bigr|.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{n}\}_{n=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{n}\}_{n=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be the
Markov chain on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> with forward transition kernels
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}:\Sigma^{q}\times\Sigma^{q}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}:\Sigma^{q}\times\Sigma^{q}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fix a noise reference distribution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and assume that for some
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{T}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{T}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\mu_{T}-\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\delta_{T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\mu_{T}-\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\delta_{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}\}_{n=0}^{T-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}\}_{n=0}^{T-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a well-defined family of reverse kernels that satisfy (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Reverse Process ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Consider approximate reverse kernels
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\widehat{k}_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}\}_{n=0}^{T-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\widehat{k}_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}\}_{n=0}^{T-1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0,\dots,T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0,\dots,T-1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}}\left\lVert\widehat{k}_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\cdot,\sigma)-k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\cdot,\sigma)\right\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\eta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}}\left\lVert\widehat{k}_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\cdot,\sigma)-k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\cdot,\sigma)\right\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\eta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Initialize the approximate reverse chain with the noise reference, i.e. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{T}\sim\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{T}\sim\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mu}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mu}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the law of the output <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by applying
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{k}_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}},\dots,\widehat{k}_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mi>rev</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{k}_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}},\dots,\widehat{k}_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the output distribution satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\hat{\mu}_{0}-\mu_{0}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\;\leq\;\underbrace{\delta_{T}}_{\text{Mixing error}}\;+\;\underbrace{T\,\eta.}_{\text{Reverse kernel estimation error}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Mixing error</mtext></munder><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Reverse kernel estimation error</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\hat{\mu}_{0}-\mu_{0}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\;\leq\;\underbrace{\delta_{T}}_{\text{Mixing error}}\;+\;\underbrace{T\,\eta.}_{\text{Reverse kernel estimation error}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ Reverse Process ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> bounds the error of the approximate reverse chain when
initialized from the true noise distribution <math id="S3.SSx1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof is provided in the Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Theory ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. In practice,
however, the reverse process is initialized from an empirical approximation <math id="S3.SSx1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\delta_{X^{\rm data}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>data</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\delta_{X^{\rm data}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S3.SSx1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The following corollary shows that this additional source of
error contributes additively to the final bound and captures the effect of sampling error from the noise distribution. It partially explains why masked diffusion models have been observed to perform better <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santos et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> in practice, than when the noise distribution is uniform.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Initialization error)

In the setting of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ Reverse Process ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, let
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>noise</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math> be any distribution on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for
some <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl\|\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}-\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\bigr\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\gamma." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>noise</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\|\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}-\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\bigr\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\gamma.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Initialize the approximate reverse chain with
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{T}\sim\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>noise</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{T}\sim\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mu}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mu}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
law of the output <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by applying
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{k}_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}},\dots,\widehat{k}_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mi>rev</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{k}_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}},\dots,\widehat{k}_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the output distribution satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bigl\|\widetilde{\mu}_{0}-\mu_{0}\bigr\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\;\leq\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bigl\|\widetilde{\mu}_{0}-\mu_{0}\bigr\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\;\leq\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{\delta_{T}}_{\text{Mixing Error}}+\underbrace{T\eta}_{\text{Reverse kernel estimation error}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Mixing Error</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Reverse kernel estimation error</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{\delta_{T}}_{\text{Mixing Error}}+\underbrace{T\eta}_{\text{Reverse kernel estimation error}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\underbrace{\gamma.}_{\text{Noise sampling error}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Noise sampling error</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\underbrace{\gamma.}_{\text{Noise sampling error}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">One can use this corollary to see the effect of error due to sampling from the noise distribution. For instance, let <math id="S3.SSx1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\delta_{X^{\rm noise}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>noise</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>noise</mi></msubsup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\delta_{X^{\rm noise}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the approximating empirical distribution based on
<math id="S3.SSx1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. samples <math id="S3.SSx1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X^{\rm noise}_{1},...X^{\rm noise}_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>noise</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>noise</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X^{\rm noise}_{1},...X^{\rm noise}_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SSx1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then from results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berend and Kontorovich, 2012</a>)</cite> one can quantify the effect of sampling error from the noise distribution, on the distance of the sampled distribution from the data distribution. In the special case, when <math id="S3.SSx1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\rm noise}=\delta_{\sigma_{\rm mask}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>mask</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\rm noise}=\delta_{\sigma_{\rm mask}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.SSx1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\rm mask}\in\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>mask</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\rm mask}\in\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, then it is to see that one can in fact, take a stronger bound by setting <math id="S3.SSx1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0</annotation></semantics></math>. While this might explain partially why diffusion models with absorbing states perform better as observed in literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santos et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, it can be that the estimation error of the reversal kernel, as captured by <math id="S3.SSx1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is high in such situations, as the distribution becomes much more concentrated. On the other hand, in experiments, we observed uniform distribution performed better. We conjecture this is due to this noising process increasing the temperature of the distribution and due to fact that higher-temperature distributions being easier to learn via NeurISE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jayakumar et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SSx1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark: non-uniqueness of reverse process.</span>
Another point to note is that the reverse process kernel that achieves the marginals <math id="S3.SSx1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is not unique, and there may exist reverse kernels different from <math id="S3.SSx1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\rm rev}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\rm rev}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S3.Ex2" title="In Reverse Process ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>); see Section <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Non-uniqueness of Reverse Processes ‣ Appendix C Theory ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>. This is an important observation, since some prior works, such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, explicitly derive the time-reversal of a prescribed forward process but, during training, learn a parametric reverse process that only needs to reproduce the target marginals rather than coincide with the exact time-reversed kernel. Consequently, the learned reverse dynamics may not correspond to the <em id="S3.SSx1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">canonical time-reversal</em> of the forward process derived earlier in this section, even when they generate the correct marginal distributions. This is different from the situation considered in this paper and in works such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campbell et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, where the loss function explicitly enforces this canonical choice. For comparison, in the continuous space, where SDEs are used to noise and denoise, the canonical choice is the one considered in time-reversal theorems in the stochastic processes literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anderson, 1982</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haussmann and Pardoux, 1986</a>)</cite>. On the other hand, the probability flow ODE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2020</a>)</cite> provides a non-canonical time reversal.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Round-Robin Forward Noising</h3>

<div id="S3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now describe a choice of forward dynamics, according to a noising scheme introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Varma et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, that will be used in this paper. In this choice of the forward process, we gradually introduce noise into the configuration by modifying one coordinate (e.g., a pixel or spin) at a time. An advantage of this form of noising is that the number of ratios that are required to be learned for each time step is much smaller, than with other schemes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, where all the variables are noised simultaneously. The precise scheme for noising is the following:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A randomization parameter <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At each time step <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{1,\dots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{1,\dots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math>, a specific coordinate is selected in round-robin order: the <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is selected as <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=((n-1)\mod q)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=((n-1)\mod q)+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With probability <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> the coordinate <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is left unchanged. With probability <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> the coordinate value <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly randomly sampled from <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since there are <math id="S3.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> number of elements in <math id="S3.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, the conditional probabilities if this forward noising process are given by:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})=\begin{cases}\frac{1-\varepsilon}{p},&amp;\text{if }\sigma_{-u}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-u},~\sigma_{u}\neq\tilde{\sigma}_{u},\\
\frac{1-\varepsilon}{p}+\varepsilon,&amp;\text{if }\sigma=\tilde{\sigma},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})=\begin{cases}\frac{1-\varepsilon}{p},&amp;\text{if }\sigma_{-u}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-u},~\sigma_{u}\neq\tilde{\sigma}_{u},\\
\frac{1-\varepsilon}{p}+\varepsilon,&amp;\text{if }\sigma=\tilde{\sigma},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SSx2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{-u}\in\Sigma^{q-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{-u}\in\Sigma^{q-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the configuration excluding the
<math id="S3.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate.
For notational convinience in the forthcoming expressions, we define the parameters,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a=\frac{1-\varepsilon}{p},~~b=\frac{1-\varepsilon}{p}+\varepsilon," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo rspace="0.827em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a=\frac{1-\varepsilon}{p},~~b=\frac{1-\varepsilon}{p}+\varepsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SSx2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">the probability of noising the chosen coordinate to an alphabet different from its current value, and the probability of it picking the current alphabet again, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the only admissible transitions are one coordinate transition away at the noised coordinate <math id="S3.SSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=((n-1)\mod q)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=((n-1)\mod q)+1</annotation></semantics></math>, we can substitute the expression for <math id="S3.SSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.Ex2" title="In Reverse Process ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) to express the reverse kernel as,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{a\,\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{a\,\mu_{n}(\sigma)+b\,\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})+a\!\!\!\sum\limits_{\hat{\sigma}\in\mathcal{N}_{u}(\tilde{\sigma})\setminus\{\sigma,\tilde{\sigma}\}}\!\!\!\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.186em;" width="0.186em"><mi>a</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{a\,\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{a\,\mu_{n}(\sigma)+b\,\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})+a\!\!\!\sum\limits_{\hat{\sigma}\in\mathcal{N}_{u}(\tilde{\sigma})\setminus\{\sigma,\tilde{\sigma}\}}\!\!\!\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{u}(\tilde{\sigma}):=\{\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}:\hat{\sigma}_{-u}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-u}\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{u}(\tilde{\sigma}):=\{\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}:\hat{\sigma}_{-u}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-u}\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Therefore, this gives rise to three possibilities,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{a\,\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{b\,\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})+a\sum\limits_{\mathcal{N}_{u}(\tilde{\sigma})\setminus\{\tilde{\sigma}\}}\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{a\,\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{b\,\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})+a\sum\limits_{\mathcal{N}_{u}(\tilde{\sigma})\setminus\{\tilde{\sigma}\}}\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SSx2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">when <math id="S3.SSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{-u}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-u},\ \text{but}\ \ \sigma\neq\tilde{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mtext>but</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{-u}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-u},\ \text{but}\ \ \sigma\neq\tilde{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma,\sigma)=\frac{b\,\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{b\,\mu_{n}(\sigma)+a\sum\limits_{\mathcal{N}_{u}(\sigma)\setminus\{\sigma\}}\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma,\sigma)=\frac{b\,\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{b\,\mu_{n}(\sigma)+a\sum\limits_{\mathcal{N}_{u}(\sigma)\setminus\{\sigma\}}\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SSx2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S3.SSx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})=0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
Of special interest is the case when the discrete set is binary: <math id="S3.SSx2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma:=\{-1,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma:=\{-1,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is binary. Then, we get,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})=\begin{cases}\frac{1-\varepsilon}{2},&amp;\text{if }\sigma_{-u}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-u},~\sigma_{u}\neq\tilde{\sigma}_{u},\\
\frac{1+\varepsilon}{2},&amp;\text{if }\sigma=\tilde{\sigma},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})=\begin{cases}\frac{1-\varepsilon}{2},&amp;\text{if }\sigma_{-u}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-u},~\sigma_{u}\neq\tilde{\sigma}_{u},\\
\frac{1+\varepsilon}{2},&amp;\text{if }\sigma=\tilde{\sigma},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The corresponding reverse transition probabilities are:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{n}^{\text{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{n}^{\text{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(1-\varepsilon)\cdot\frac{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{(1-\varepsilon)\mu_{n}(\sigma)+(1+\varepsilon)\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(1-\varepsilon)\cdot\frac{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{(1-\varepsilon)\mu_{n}(\sigma)+(1+\varepsilon)\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\text{if }\sigma_{-u}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-u}\ \text{and}\ ~\sigma_{u}\neq\tilde{\sigma}_{u}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0.830em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\text{if }\sigma_{-u}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-u}\ \text{and}\ ~\sigma_{u}\neq\tilde{\sigma}_{u},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SSx2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{n}^{\text{rev}}(\sigma,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{n}^{\text{rev}}(\sigma,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(1+\varepsilon)\cdot\frac{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{(1+\varepsilon)\mu_{n}(\sigma)+(1-\varepsilon)\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(1+\varepsilon)\cdot\frac{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{(1+\varepsilon)\mu_{n}(\sigma)+(1-\varepsilon)\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SSx2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Hard Noise Autoregressive Limit</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We now consider a special case of the forward process in which noise is <span id="S3.SSx2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">harsh</span>: <math id="S3.SSx2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=0</annotation></semantics></math>. This corresponds to
a full randomization of the selected coordinate at each step, and hence all the information is lost in the variable after the corresponding noising step. Our goal in this section is to show that we recover auto-regressive generation in this limit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SSx2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=q</annotation></semantics></math>, and define a time-inhomogeneous Markov kernel that updates only the
<math id="S3.SSx2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate at time step <math id="S3.SSx2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{1,\ldots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{1,\ldots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The forward transition kernel simplifies to,</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{p},&amp;\text{if }\sigma_{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n},\\[6.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{p},&amp;\text{if }\sigma_{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n},\\[6.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Over <math id="S3.SSx2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=p</annotation></semantics></math> steps, this procedure fully randomizes each coordinate once,
resulting in convergence to the uniform distribution over <math id="S3.SSx2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">By the reverse kernel construction, we obtain</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(X_{n}=\sigma\mid X_{n+1}=\tilde{\sigma})=k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(X_{n}=\sigma\mid X_{n+1}=\tilde{\sigma})=k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{1}_{\sigma_{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}}\cdot\frac{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\mathcal{N}_{n}(\sigma)}\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{1}_{\sigma_{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}}\cdot\frac{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\mathcal{N}_{n}(\sigma)}\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">This corresponds to sampling the <math id="S3.SSx2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate conditioned on the others:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(X_{n}^{n}=\sigma_{n}\mid X_{n}^{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n})=\frac{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\mathcal{N}_{n}(\sigma)}\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(X_{n}^{n}=\sigma_{n}\mid X_{n}^{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n})=\frac{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}{\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\mathcal{N}_{n}(\sigma)}\mu_{n}(\hat{\sigma})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SSx2.p11.2" class="ltx_p">where the sum is taken over all configurations
<math id="S3.SSx2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> that agree with <math id="S3.SSx2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> outside coordinate <math id="S3.SSx2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We now express this reverse process recursively.
Let <math id="S3.SSx2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}=\tilde{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}=\tilde{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> be a configuration sampled from the noise distribution.
At each reverse step <math id="S3.SSx2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the process samples a configuration <math id="S3.SSx2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}=\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}=\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S3.SSx2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}</annotation></semantics></math>, while drawing <math id="S3.SSx2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
from the corresponding conditional distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Formally,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(X_{n}=\sigma\mid X_{n+1}=\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(X_{n}=\sigma\mid X_{n+1}=\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{1}_{\sigma_{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}}\cdot\mathbb{P}(X_{n}^{n}=\sigma_{n}\mid X_{n}^{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{1}_{\sigma_{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}}\cdot\mathbb{P}(X_{n}^{n}=\sigma_{n}\mid X_{n}^{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Unrolling the reverse chain from <math id="S3.SSx2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SSx2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> yields</p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(X_{0}=\sigma\mid X_{T}=\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(X_{0}=\sigma\mid X_{T}=\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\prod_{n=1}^{T}\mathbb{P}(X_{T-n}=\sigma_{T-n}\mid X_{T-n+1}=\tilde{\sigma}_{T-n+1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\prod_{n=1}^{T}\mathbb{P}(X_{T-n}=\sigma_{T-n}\mid X_{T-n+1}=\tilde{\sigma}_{T-n+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Since the two configurations differ only at coordinate <math id="S3.SSx2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(X_{T-n}=\sigma\mid X_{T-n+1}=\tilde{\sigma})=\mathbf{1}_{\sigma_{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}}\,\mathbb{P}(\sigma_{n}\mid\tilde{\sigma}_{-n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(X_{T-n}=\sigma\mid X_{T-n+1}=\tilde{\sigma})=\mathbf{1}_{\sigma_{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}}\,\mathbb{P}(\sigma_{n}\mid\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting into (<a href="#S3.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ Round-Robin Forward Noising ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), we obtain</p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(X_{0}=\sigma\mid X_{T}=\tilde{\sigma})=\prod_{n=1}^{T}\mathbf{1}_{\sigma_{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}}\,\mathbb{P}(\sigma_{n}\mid\tilde{\sigma}_{-n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(X_{0}=\sigma\mid X_{T}=\tilde{\sigma})=\prod_{n=1}^{T}\mathbf{1}_{\sigma_{-n}=\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}}\,\mathbb{P}(\sigma_{n}\mid\tilde{\sigma}_{-n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SSx2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SSx2.p17.1" class="ltx_p">If the reverse update keeps non-updated coordinates fixed, i.e. <math id="S3.SSx2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}^{-n}=X_{n+1}^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}^{-n}=X_{n+1}^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the conditioning simplifies to</p>
<table id="S3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(X_{0}=\sigma\mid X_{T}=\tilde{\sigma})=\prod_{n=1}^{T}\mathbb{P}(\sigma_{n}\mid\tilde{\sigma}_{&gt;n})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(X_{0}=\sigma\mid X_{T}=\tilde{\sigma})=\prod_{n=1}^{T}\mathbb{P}(\sigma_{n}\mid\tilde{\sigma}_{&gt;n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SSx2.p17.2" class="ltx_p">which recovers an autoregressive factorization over the discrete alphabet
<math id="S3.SSx2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Learning Conditionals using Neural Interaction Screening</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the probability distribution of the random variable <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In order to implement the reverse dynamics, we need to estimate the ratio <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}</annotation></semantics></math>
from samples of the forward process. Suppose <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma,\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma,\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> differ at only one coordinate <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\sigma}_{i}=\sigma_{i}\quad\text{for all }i\neq n,\quad\tilde{\sigma}_{n}\neq\sigma_{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\sigma}_{i}=\sigma_{i}\quad\text{for all }i\neq n,\quad\tilde{\sigma}_{n}\neq\sigma_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then, for any distribution <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}=\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma}_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}=\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma}_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{-n}\in\Sigma^{q-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{-n}\in\Sigma^{q-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the shared values of the configuration outside the <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This identity follows directly from the definition of conditional probability
<math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n}(\sigma)=\mu_{n}(\sigma_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})\cdot\mu_{n}(\sigma_{-n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n}(\sigma)=\mu_{n}(\sigma_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})\cdot\mu_{n}(\sigma_{-n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Taking the ratio, we obtain:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}=\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma}_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})\cdot\mu_{n}(\sigma_{-n})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})\cdot\mu_{n}(\sigma_{-n})}=\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma}_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}=\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma}_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})\cdot\mu_{n}(\sigma_{-n})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})\cdot\mu_{n}(\sigma_{-n})}=\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma}_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma_{n}\mid\sigma_{-n})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This expression provides a tractable way to compute (or approximate) the required ratio using only local conditionals for the revese dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To compute the required single-site conditional distributions, we use the
<em id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Neural Interaction Screening Estimator</em> (NeurISE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jayakumar et al., 2020</a>)</cite>,
which learns local conditionals in discrete graphical models by neural
parameterization of partial energy functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This local conditional modeling is well matched to the reverse diffusion kernel,
which depends only on ratios of single-site conditionals between configurations
differing at one coordinate, enabling efficient and scalable implementation of
the reverse-time dynamics without explicitly modeling the global distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Following NeurISE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jayakumar et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, we introduce the centered indicator embedding</p>
<table id="S4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{s}(r):=\begin{cases}1-\dfrac{1}{q},&amp;r=s,\\[6.0pt]
-\dfrac{1}{q},&amp;r\neq s,\end{cases}\qquad s,r\in\Sigma." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{s}(r):=\begin{cases}1-\dfrac{1}{q},&amp;r=s,\\[6.0pt]
-\dfrac{1}{q},&amp;r\neq s,\end{cases}\qquad s,r\in\Sigma.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">We then define the vector-valued embedding, <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(r):=\bigl(\Phi_{1}(r),\ldots,\Phi_{q}(r)\bigr)\in\mathbb{R}^{q}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(r):=\bigl(\Phi_{1}(r),\ldots,\Phi_{q}(r)\bigr)\in\mathbb{R}^{q},</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that
<math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n}(\sigma)\propto\exp(H(\sigma))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n}(\sigma)\propto\exp(H(\sigma))</annotation></semantics></math> is a Gibbs distribution for some Hamiltonian function
<math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:\Sigma^{q}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:\Sigma^{q}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any coordinate <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u\in[1,,,q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in[1,,,q]</annotation></semantics></math>, there always exists a decomposition <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\sigma)=H_{-u}(\sigma_{-u})+H_{u}(\sigma)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\sigma)=H_{-u}(\sigma_{-u})+H_{u}(\sigma),</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{-u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{-u}</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>.w
Substituting it into the Gibbs distribution
<math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n}(\sigma)\propto\exp(H(\sigma))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n}(\sigma)\propto\exp(H(\sigma))</annotation></semantics></math> yields</p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu_{n}(\sigma_{u}\mid\sigma_{-u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu_{n}(\sigma_{u}\mid\sigma_{-u})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\exp\!\left(H_{-u}(\sigma_{-u})+H_{u}(\sigma)\right)}{\sum_{r\in\Sigma}\exp\!\left(H_{-u}(\sigma_{-u})+H_{u}(\sigma_{u}=r,\sigma_{-u})\right)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\exp\!\left(H_{-u}(\sigma_{-u})+H_{u}(\sigma)\right)}{\sum_{r\in\Sigma}\exp\!\left(H_{-u}(\sigma_{-u})+H_{u}(\sigma_{u}=r,\sigma_{-u})\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{-u}(\sigma_{-u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{-u}(\sigma_{-u})</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get</p>
<table id="S4.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{n}(\sigma_{u}\mid\sigma_{-u})=\frac{\exp\!\left(H_{u}(\sigma)\right)}{\sum_{r\in\Sigma}\exp\!\left(H_{u}(\sigma_{u}=r,\sigma_{-u})\right)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{n}(\sigma_{u}\mid\sigma_{-u})=\frac{\exp\!\left(H_{u}(\sigma)\right)}{\sum_{r\in\Sigma}\exp\!\left(H_{u}(\sigma_{u}=r,\sigma_{-u})\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.3" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the partial energy <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> determines the single-site conditional
distribution up to an additive function of <math id="S4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{-u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{-u}</annotation></semantics></math>, and can be written as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}(\sigma)=\log\mu_{n}(\sigma_{u}\mid\sigma_{-u})+\mathrm{const}(\sigma_{-u})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>const</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}(\sigma)=\log\mu_{n}(\sigma_{u}\mid\sigma_{-u})+\mathrm{const}(\sigma_{-u}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.4" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S4.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{-u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{-u}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}(\cdot,\sigma_{-u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}(\cdot,\sigma_{-u})</annotation></semantics></math> can chosen be to satisfy <math id="S4.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{r\in\Sigma}H_{u}(r,\sigma_{-u})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{r\in\Sigma}H_{u}(r,\sigma_{-u})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The functions <math id="S4.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> form a basis for functions, <math id="S4.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Sigma\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Sigma\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> that average to <math id="S4.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or are <span id="S4.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">centered</span>.
Therefore, for each coordinate <math id="S4.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in[q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in[q]</annotation></semantics></math>, we approximate this partial energy using a
neural network <math id="S4.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{NN}_{\theta}:\Sigma^{q-1}\to\mathbb{R}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>NN</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{NN}_{\theta}:\Sigma^{q-1}\to\mathbb{R}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>
Specifically, we use this parameterization at the Hamiltonian level as follows,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widetilde{H}_{u}(\sigma;w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widetilde{H}_{u}(\sigma;w)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\langle\Phi(\sigma_{u}),\mathrm{NN}_{\theta}(\sigma_{-u})\bigr\rangle=\sum_{s=1}^{q}\Phi_{s}(\sigma_{u})\,\mathrm{NN}_{\theta}(\sigma_{-u})_{s}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>NN</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>q</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>NN</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\langle\Phi(\sigma_{u}),\mathrm{NN}_{\theta}(\sigma_{-u})\bigr\rangle=\sum_{s=1}^{q}\Phi_{s}(\sigma_{u})\,\mathrm{NN}_{\theta}(\sigma_{-u})_{s}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.5" class="ltx_p">This representation is fully general without loss of expressivity.
Given samples <math id="S4.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\sigma^{(n)}\}_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\sigma^{(n)}\}_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> from the forward process at time <math id="S4.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S4.p8.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">NeurISE loss</em> as presented in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jayakumar et al., 2020</a>)</cite>,
for site <math id="S4.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S4.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{u}(\theta)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\exp\!\left(-\bigl\langle\Phi(\sigma_{u}^{(n)}),\mathrm{NN}_{\theta}(\sigma_{-u}^{(n)})\bigr\rangle\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>NN</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{u}(\theta)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\exp\!\left(-\bigl\langle\Phi(\sigma_{u}^{(n)}),\mathrm{NN}_{\theta}(\sigma_{-u}^{(n)})\bigr\rangle\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> represents the trainable parameters of the neural net</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Since we have conditionals that need to be learned be for each time step, we introduce a neural network <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NN_{\theta}:\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{q}\times\mathbb{R}^{q-1}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NN_{\theta}:\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{q}\times\mathbb{R}^{q-1}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> that accepts arguments <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,u,\sigma_{-u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,u,\sigma_{-u})</annotation></semantics></math> where the coordinate <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is encoded as a one-hot vector. This gives us the composite loss</p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{aligned} &amp;\mathcal{L}_{u}(\theta)=\\
&amp;\frac{1}{TN}\sum_{s=1}^{T}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\exp\!\left(-\bigl\langle\Phi((X^{n}_{s})_{u}),\mathrm{NN}_{\theta}(t,u,(X^{n}_{s})_{-u}\bigr\rangle\right).\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>NN</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><mo>)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{aligned} &amp;\mathcal{L}_{u}(\theta)=\\
&amp;\frac{1}{TN}\sum_{s=1}^{T}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\exp\!\left(-\bigl\langle\Phi((X^{n}_{s})_{u}),\mathrm{NN}_{\theta}(t,u,(X^{n}_{s})_{-u}\bigr\rangle\right).\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Learned Conditional Distribution.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once trained, the approximate conditional distribution is recovered by setting</p>
<table id="S4.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mu}_{n}(\sigma_{u}\mid\sigma_{-u})=\frac{\exp\!\left(\bigl\langle\Phi(\sigma_{u}),\mathrm{NN}_{u}(\sigma_{-u})\bigr\rangle\right)}{\sum_{r\in\Sigma}\exp\!\left(\bigl\langle\Phi(r),\mathrm{NN}_{u}(\sigma_{-u})\bigr\rangle\right)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>NN</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>NN</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mu}_{n}(\sigma_{u}\mid\sigma_{-u})=\frac{\exp\!\left(\bigl\langle\Phi(\sigma_{u}),\mathrm{NN}_{u}(\sigma_{-u})\bigr\rangle\right)}{\sum_{r\in\Sigma}\exp\!\left(\bigl\langle\Phi(r),\mathrm{NN}_{u}(\sigma_{-u})\bigr\rangle\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore, for any pair <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> differing at coordinate <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, the ratio required
for the reverse diffusion kernel is given by</p>
<table id="S4.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}\;\approx\;\frac{\exp\!\left(\bigl\langle\Phi(\tilde{\sigma}_{u}),\mathrm{NN}_{u}(\sigma_{-u})\bigr\rangle\right)}{\exp\!\left(\bigl\langle\Phi(\sigma_{u}),\mathrm{NN}_{u}(\sigma_{-u})\bigr\rangle\right)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>NN</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>NN</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mu_{n}(\tilde{\sigma})}{\mu_{n}(\sigma)}\;\approx\;\frac{\exp\!\left(\bigl\langle\Phi(\tilde{\sigma}_{u}),\mathrm{NN}_{u}(\sigma_{-u})\bigr\rangle\right)}{\exp\!\left(\bigl\langle\Phi(\sigma_{u}),\mathrm{NN}_{u}(\sigma_{-u})\bigr\rangle\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Numerical Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20293v1/TV_opt_pdf.svg" id="S5.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/318;" width="476" height="318"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TV</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20293v1/CC_opt_pdf.svg" id="S5.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/318;" width="476" height="318"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Cross-correlation Error</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F1.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20293v1/Train_opt_pdf.svg" id="S5.F1.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/318;" width="476" height="318"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S5.F1.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training Time</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Trend of TV and Cross-correlation error as a function of training set size for Ising models, averaged over <math id="S5.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> Ising models and <math id="S5.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> trials per data set. The test sample size was taken to be <math id="S5.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> for each experiment. Error bars represent one standard deviation over trials.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we compare our denoising method that we refer to as <em id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">NeurISE diffusion</em>, with two representative ELBO- and score-based methods proposed in the literature: D3PM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and the score matching approach (SEDD) proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. The relevant code used to run the experiments can be found in <a href="https://github.com/lanl-ansi/NeurISEdiffusion" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Github repository</a>. We implemented our own version of SEDD, and the D3PM implementation was adapted from an unofficial publicly available implementation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ryu, 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The core parametric model we will use in our method and each of these methods will be multilayer perceptrons with different depth, depending on the test cases, with batch normalization layers. We make this choice to study the performance of these methods on an equal footing, decoupled from representational differences coming from the model.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Test Case 1: Edwards-Anderson Model</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first compare different methods on small-scale synthetic data. The benchmark is based on the <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Edwards-Anderson (EA) model</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edwards and Anderson, 1975</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt and Young, 1988</a>)</cite>, which is a specific instance of the binary Ising model defined by the Hamiltonian in Equation (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2 Problem ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The EA model Hamiltonian is defined over <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> binary variables <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}\in\{-1,+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}\in\{-1,+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, arranged on a two-dimensional square lattice of size <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\times L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\times L</annotation></semantics></math>. The graph <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subseteq[q]\times[q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subseteq[q]\times[q]</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the set of nearest-neighbor pairs on a 2D periodic grid. Each spin <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> interacts with its right and bottom neighbors, with periodic boundary conditions applied in both directions. The pairwise couplings <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> are symmetric random variables sampled independently for each edge <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)\in E</annotation></semantics></math> as:
<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{ij}=J_{ji}\in\{-1.2,+1.2\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{ij}=J_{ji}\in\{-1.2,+1.2\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The local fields <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\in\{-0.05,+0.05\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\in\{-0.05,+0.05\}</annotation></semantics></math> are also sampled independently for each node <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[q]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we use a lattice size of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=5</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in a model with <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=25</annotation></semantics></math> binary variables. The results of using a two layer MLP can be seen for different diffusion methods in Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5 Numerical Experiments ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We test the models for different values of training data across averaged over <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> different choices of EA models, with <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> runs for differing values of training set size: <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[100,320,1000...10^{5}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>100,320</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[100,320,1000...10^{5}]</annotation></semantics></math>. The training samples were generated using an exact sampler. The number of test samples were fixed to be <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> across all three experiments. We find that the NeuRISE-based denoising estimator shows the sharpest decay in total variation (TV) distance with increase in sample size, and performs better than the SEDD approach proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. For the model presented in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, the configurations had to be one-hot coded to make the algorithm work. The model D3PM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>)</cite> performs well for low number of samples but its performance deteriorates as the size of the training set is increased. Interestingly, D3PM does not show monotonic decay of TV as the number of training samples decrease. In each case, we also compute the difference between the cross-correlation matrices of the generated samples and the test data, where the correlations are defined as <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{ij}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\sigma_{i}^{(k)}\sigma_{j}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{ij}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\sigma_{i}^{(k)}\sigma_{j}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The decay of cross-correlation errors show a similar trend as that for the TV. Cross-correlation metric has the advantage of tractability for larger models where TV can’t be efficiently computed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Another important study that was performed is the comparison of the NeuRISE-based diffusion for different choice of noise parameter. From our experiments, it doesn’t seem like the denoising scheme with soft noise significantly outperform harsh noise setting, which corresponds to autoregressive generation. In fact, for small training sets, the harsh noise case uniformly performs better than the other schemes. See Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Supplementary Numerics ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Test Case 2: MNIST</h3>

<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20293v1/figs/MNIST_Neurise.png" id="S5.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/237;" width="476" height="237" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">NeurISE Diffusion</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20293v1/figs/MNIST_D3PM.png" id="S5.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/237;" width="476" height="237" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">D3PM</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20293v1/figs/MNIST_SEDD.png" id="S5.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/237;" width="476" height="237" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S5.F2.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">SEDD</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Class-conditional MNIST samples.
Each subfigure shows generated samples arranged with one row per digit (0–9).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the proposed discrete NeurISE diffusion model on the binarized MNIST dataset, which consists of grayscale images of handwritten digits. Images are discretized into a binarized alphabet by thresholding pixel intensities at a fixed midpoint value, assigning pixels to one of two categories depending on whether their intensity lies above or below the threshold. This results in binary-valued vectors in <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=784" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>784</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=784</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of pixels in each image. For this benchmark, we allowed a hyperoptimization schedule to search over MLPs of upto <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> layers. We used the MMD metric <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gretton et al., 2012</a>)</cite> and average cross-correlation of the samples, to compare the performance of the models for conditional sampling task. As can be seen in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.3 Test Case 3: D-Wave Dataset ‣ 5 Numerical Experiments ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the NeuRISE based learning of conditionals achieve the lowest MMD and cross-correlation error. Samples of generated images can be seen in Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.2 Test Case 2: MNIST ‣ 5 Numerical Experiments ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to the EA model, we see that D3PM achieves a much better comparable performance in this setting. Note that the metrics used here do not compute a worst case error between distributions as TV does. This indicates that D3PM is good at reproducing a lower-order projection of the dataset that aligns with such metrics, but struggles with true distribution learning, where NeuRISE Diffusion succeeds.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Test Case 3: D-Wave Dataset</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate our method on a scientific application with real data, we use the diffusion model to learn a binary dataset produced by D-Wave’s Advantage quantum annealer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">McGeoch and Farré, 2020</a>)</cite>. This dataset is generated by performing repeated quantum annealer runs on the D-Wave machine, with a randomized set of input Hamiltonians. For our experiments we choose <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=2000</annotation></semantics></math> qubits, which forms a subsection of the annealer and train a diffusion model on the data produced by this portion of the chip. In this example, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\times 10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times 10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> samples were used for training, and <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> samples were reserved for testing. Results in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.3 Test Case 3: D-Wave Dataset ‣ 5 Numerical Experiments ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> again show the advantage of NeurISE Diffusion in all metrics.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
