<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Algorithm</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the Neurise based denoising diffusion algorithm introduced in the paper.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Discrete Diffusion with NeurISE</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> alphabet <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Sigma|=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Sigma|=p</annotation></semantics></math>, dimension <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, steps <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> , noise <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, data distribution <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (samples <math id="alg1.l1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext=",\sigma_{0}\sim\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">,\sigma_{0}\sim\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>), Number of samples <math id="alg1.l1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Forward diffusion </span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
           Sample time index <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\{1,2,\ldots,T\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\{1,2,\ldots,T\})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
           Initialize <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\leftarrow\sigma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\leftarrow\sigma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1,2,\ldots,N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1,2,\ldots,N</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\leftarrow((t-1)\bmod q)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\leftarrow((t-1)\bmod q)+1</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
            Set <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{-u}\leftarrow\sigma_{-u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{-u}\leftarrow\sigma_{-u}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
            With probability <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> change coordinate <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> according <math id="alg1.l7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{u}\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{u}\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
           Output forward tuple <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t,\sigma_{0},\sigma_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t,\sigma_{0},\sigma_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\leftarrow\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\leftarrow\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learn conditionals with NeurISE.</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Goal:</span> estimate single-site conditionals <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{s}(\cdot\mid\sigma_{s,-u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{s}(\cdot\mid\sigma_{s,-u})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="alg1.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0,\ldots,T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0,\ldots,T-1</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0,1,\ldots,T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0,1,\ldots,T-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
            
            
            Generate noised samples at time <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> by running the forward kernel on data to obtain a batch <math id="alg1.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\sigma_{s}^{(n)}\}_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\sigma_{s}^{(n)}\}_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=1,2,\ldots,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=1,2,\ldots,q</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
            
            
             Train NeurISE network <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{NN}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>NN</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{NN}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing the NeurISE objective (<a href="#S4.E18" title="Equation 18 ‣ 4 Learning Conditionals using Neural Interaction Screening ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
            
            
             Obtain conditional estimator <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{s}(\cdot\mid\sigma_{s,-u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{s}(\cdot\mid\sigma_{s,-u})</annotation></semantics></math> via (<a href="#S4.E19" title="Equation 19 ‣ Learned Conditional Distribution. ‣ 4 Learning Conditionals using Neural Interaction Screening ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l17.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l18.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l19.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">19:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reverse sampling (denoising).</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l20.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">20:</span></span>
            
            
           Initialize <math id="alg1.l20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\sigma}_{T}\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\Sigma^{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\sigma}_{T}\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\Sigma^{q})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l21.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">21:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=T-1,T-2,\ldots,0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=T-1,T-2,\ldots,0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l21.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l22" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l22.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">22:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\leftarrow(r\bmod q)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>mod</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\leftarrow(r\bmod q)+1</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l23" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l23.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">23:</span></span>
            
            
            Set <math id="alg1.l23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}^{rev}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}^{rev}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> according to (<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ Round-Robin Forward Noising ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>)-(<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Round-Robin Forward Noising ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l24" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l24.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">24:</span></span>
            
            
            Sample <math id="alg1.l24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\sigma}_{T-1}\sim\hat{k}^{rev}_{n}(\sigma_{T},\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\sigma}_{T-1}\sim\hat{k}^{rev}_{n}(\sigma_{T},\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l25" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l25.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">25:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l25.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l26" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l26.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">26:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\sigma}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\sigma}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Supplementary Numerics</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Soft Noise vs Harsh Noise</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show our comparison of the harsh noise setting with soft noise ones, to facilitate comparison of autoregressive vs diffusion model. From our observation, the harsh noise setting performs better at low number of training samples and for large number of training samples, the models show very similar performance. See Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ B.1 Soft Noise vs Harsh Noise ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Numerics ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Here, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the total number of time-steps used in the noising and denosing phase, and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the noise parameter <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20293v1/TV_hs_pdf.svg" id="A2.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/318;" width="476" height="318"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TV</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20293v1/Train_hs_pdf.svg" id="A2.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/318;" width="476" height="318"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training Time</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="A2.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Trend of TV and Training time for Ising models, averaged <math id="A2.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> trials per data set. The test sample size was taken to be <math id="A2.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> for each experiment. The harsh noise version of the problem performs competitively with situations where noise is soft.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Local vs Global Neural Network</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we compare the performance of diffusion models trained using two architectures: (i) a collection of local neural networks, with one network per time step, and (ii) a single global neural network shared across the entire time horizon. As shown in Fig. <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ B.2 Local vs Global Neural Network ‣ Appendix B Supplementary Numerics ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, the local architecture consistently outperforms the global one, despite both approaches having approximately the same total number of trainable parameters. Specifically, in the local setting, each time step is modeled by a single-hidden-layer MLP with 5 hidden units, whereas the global model uses a fixed network of width 125.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We tested NeuRISE Diffusion for two different lattices: <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math>, each with alphabet size <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. As can be seen Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.4 Test case 4: Multi-Alphabet Potts Models ‣ 5 Numerical Experiments ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the TV decreases in a statistically expected way as the number of training samples is increased from <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20293v1/TV_lg_pdf.svg" id="A2.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/318;" width="476" height="318"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TV</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20293v1/Train_lg_pdf.svg" id="A2.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/318;" width="476" height="318"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training Time</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A2.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Trend of TV and Training time for Ising models, averaged <math id="A2.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> trials per data set. The test sample size was taken to be <math id="A2.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> for each experiment. Learning individual conditionals using separate neural networks achieves lower TV, than using one global neural network across all conditionals.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Theory</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the presentation in this section, we introduce some additional notation.
Given a probability distribution <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the action of the reverse kernel
<math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="A3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}})(\tilde{\sigma})=\sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}}\mu(\sigma)\,k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma}),\qquad\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}})(\tilde{\sigma})=\sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}}\mu(\sigma)\,k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma}),\qquad\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For two kernels <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math>, their
composition is the kernel</p>
<table id="A3.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}k_{n-1}^{\mathrm{rev}})(\sigma,\sigma^{\prime})=\sum_{\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})\,k_{n-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\tilde{\sigma},\sigma^{\prime})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}k_{n-1}^{\mathrm{rev}})(\sigma,\sigma^{\prime})=\sum_{\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})\,k_{n-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\tilde{\sigma},\sigma^{\prime}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which corresponds to applying <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math> first and
<math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math> second.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, the composed reverse kernel</p>
<table id="A3.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}=k_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdots k_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}=k_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdots k_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">satisfies</p>
<table id="A3.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}})(\sigma_{T},\sigma_{0})=\sum_{\sigma_{1},\dots,\sigma_{T-1}\in\Sigma^{q}}\prod_{t=0}^{T-1}k_{t}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma_{t+1},\sigma_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}})(\sigma_{T},\sigma_{0})=\sum_{\sigma_{1},\dots,\sigma_{T-1}\in\Sigma^{q}}\prod_{t=0}^{T-1}k_{t}^{\mathrm{rev}}(\sigma_{t+1},\sigma_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the state at time <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the state at time
<math id="A3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the distribution obtained by initializing the reverse chain from
<math id="A3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="A3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\nu_{0}(\sigma_{0})=(\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}})(\sigma_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nu_{0}(\sigma_{0})=(\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}})(\sigma_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{\sigma_{T}\in\Sigma^{q}}\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}(\sigma_{T})(\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}})(\sigma_{T},\sigma_{0})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{\sigma_{T}\in\Sigma^{q}}\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}(\sigma_{T})(\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}})(\sigma_{T},\sigma_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">See <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ Reverse Process ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A3.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the composition of the exact reverse kernels
<math id="A3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}},\dots,k_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>rev</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}},\dots,k_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math> (applied in this order), and let
<math id="A3.p6.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{K}}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{K}}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the composition of the approximate reverse kernels
<math id="A3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{k}_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}},\dots,\widehat{k}_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mi>rev</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{k}_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}},\dots,\widehat{k}_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the law of the output obtained by running the <em id="A3.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exact</em> reverse chain initialized at
time <math id="A3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nu_{0}:=\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\,\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>noise</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}:=\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\,\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since the kernels <math id="A3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{k_{n}^{\mathrm{rev}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Reverse Process ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), initializing the exact reverse chain
from <math id="A3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="A3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu_{0}=\mu_{T}\,\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}=\mu_{T}\,\mathcal{K}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">By the data processing inequality</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\nu_{0}-\mu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\lVert\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}-\mu_{T}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\delta_{T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\nu_{0}-\mu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\lVert\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}-\mu_{T}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\delta_{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mu}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mu}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the law of the output of the <em id="A3.p8.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">approximate</em> reverse chain initialized from
<math id="A3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\mu}_{0}:=\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\,\widehat{\mathcal{K}}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>noise</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mu}_{0}:=\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\,\widehat{\mathcal{K}}_{0:T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">We bound <math id="A3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\widehat{\mu}_{0}-\nu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\widehat{\mu}_{0}-\nu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}</annotation></semantics></math> by a telescoping argument as is used in pertubation theory of Markov chains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rudolf et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Define intermediate distributions for <math id="A3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0,1,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0,1,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\rho^{(m)}:=\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\,\widehat{k}_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdots\widehat{k}_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}\,k_{T-m-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdots k_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>noise</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{(m)}:=\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\,\widehat{k}_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdots\widehat{k}_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}\,k_{T-m-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdots k_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">with the convention that <math id="A3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{(0)}=\nu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{(0)}=\nu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{(T)}=\widehat{\mu}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{(T)}=\widehat{\mu}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by the triangle inequality,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\widehat{\mu}_{0}-\nu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}=\lVert\rho^{(T)}-\rho^{(0)}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\sum_{m=1}^{T}\lVert\rho^{(m)}-\rho^{(m-1)}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\widehat{\mu}_{0}-\nu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}=\lVert\rho^{(T)}-\rho^{(0)}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\sum_{m=1}^{T}\lVert\rho^{(m)}-\rho^{(m-1)}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="A3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{1,\dots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{1,\dots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math> and set</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha^{(m)}:=\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\,\widehat{k}_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdots\widehat{k}_{T-m+1}^{\mathrm{rev}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>noise</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{(m)}:=\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\,\widehat{k}_{T-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdots\widehat{k}_{T-m+1}^{\mathrm{rev}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p8.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p8.5.1" class="ltx_text">so that <math id="A3.p8.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\rho^{(m)}=\alpha^{(m)}\widehat{k}_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}k_{T-m-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdots k_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{(m)}=\alpha^{(m)}\widehat{k}_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}k_{T-m-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdots k_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A3.p8.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\rho^{(m-1)}=\alpha^{(m)}k_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}k_{T-m-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdots k_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{(m-1)}=\alpha^{(m)}k_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}k_{T-m-1}^{\mathrm{rev}}\cdots k_{0}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using contraction of TV under a common kernel,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\rho^{(m)}-\rho^{(m-1)}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\left\lVert\alpha^{(m)}\widehat{k}_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}-\alpha^{(m)}k_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}\right\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\rho^{(m)}-\rho^{(m-1)}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\left\lVert\alpha^{(m)}\widehat{k}_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}-\alpha^{(m)}k_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}\right\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p8.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p8.6.1" class="ltx_text">For any distribution <math id="A3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and kernels <math id="A3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\alpha P-\alpha Q\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\sup_{\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}}\lVert P(\cdot,\sigma)-Q(\cdot,\sigma)\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\alpha P-\alpha Q\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\sup_{\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}}\lVert P(\cdot,\sigma)-Q(\cdot,\sigma)\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p8.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p8.7.1" class="ltx_text">Applying this with <math id="A3.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\alpha^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\alpha^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\widehat{k}_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\widehat{k}_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=k_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=k_{T-m}^{\mathrm{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the estimation error, we get,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\rho^{(m)}-\rho^{(m-1)}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\eta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\rho^{(m)}-\rho^{(m-1)}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\eta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p8.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p8.8.1" class="ltx_text">Combining gives</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\widehat{\mu}_{0}-\nu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq T\eta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\widehat{\mu}_{0}-\nu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq T\eta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, by the triangle inequality,</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lVert\widehat{\mu}_{0}-\mu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lVert\widehat{\mu}_{0}-\mu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\lVert\widehat{\mu}_{0}-\nu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}+\lVert\nu_{0}-\mu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\lVert\widehat{\mu}_{0}-\nu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}+\lVert\nu_{0}-\mu_{0}\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq T\eta+\delta_{T}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq T\eta+\delta_{T},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">which concludes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">See <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 3.2. ‣ Reverse Process ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A3.p11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{K}}^{\mathrm{rev}}_{0:T-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{K}}^{\mathrm{rev}}_{0:T-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the composition of the
approximate reverse kernels. Define</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\mu}_{0}:=\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\,\widehat{\mathcal{K}}^{\mathrm{rev}}_{0:T-1},\qquad\widetilde{\mu}_{0}:=\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}\,\widehat{\mathcal{K}}^{\mathrm{rev}}_{0:T-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>noise</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>noise</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mu}_{0}:=\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\,\widehat{\mathcal{K}}^{\mathrm{rev}}_{0:T-1},\qquad\widetilde{\mu}_{0}:=\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}\,\widehat{\mathcal{K}}^{\mathrm{rev}}_{0:T-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">The TV norm under the action of a Markov kernel <math id="A3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> remains preserved (this follows trivially from <math id="A3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}}Q(\sigma,\tilde{sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}}Q(\sigma,\tilde{sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> = 1) and hence,</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bigl\|\widetilde{\mu}_{0}-\widehat{\mu}_{0}\bigr\|_{\mathrm{TV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bigl\|\widetilde{\mu}_{0}-\widehat{\mu}_{0}\bigr\|_{\mathrm{TV}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex51.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigl\|(\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}-\mu_{\mathrm{noise}})\widehat{\mathcal{K}}^{\mathrm{rev}}_{0:T-1}\bigr\|_{\mathrm{TV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>noise</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>rev</mi></msubsup><msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em">∥</mo><mi>TV</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigl\|(\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}-\mu_{\mathrm{noise}})\widehat{\mathcal{K}}^{\mathrm{rev}}_{0:T-1}\bigr\|_{\mathrm{TV}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\bigl\|\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}-\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\bigr\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\gamma." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>noise</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>noise</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\bigl\|\widehat{\mu}_{\mathrm{noise}}-\mu_{\mathrm{noise}}\bigr\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\gamma.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p11.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p11.3.1" class="ltx_text">The claim follows by the triangle inequality together with
Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ Reverse Process ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, which gives
<math id="A3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\widehat{\mu}_{0}-\mu_{0}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\delta_{T}+T\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\widehat{\mu}_{0}-\mu_{0}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\leq\delta_{T}+T\eta</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Non-uniqueness of Reverse Processes</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we highlight that, in general, the reverse process associated with a forward Markov chain is not unique. Even when the marginal distributions <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at each time <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\{0,\dots,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\{0,\dots,T\}</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed, there may exist multiple valid reverse dynamics that recover the same marginals.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be a forward Markov process over <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}\sim\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}\sim\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The canonical construction of the reverse process introduced in the main text uses Bayes’ rule:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(X_{t}=\sigma\mid X_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(X_{t}=\sigma\mid X_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex53.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\mathbb{P}(X_{t}=\sigma,X_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma})}{\mathbb{P}(X_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\mathbb{P}(X_{t}=\sigma,X_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma})}{\mathbb{P}(X_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\mathbb{P}(X_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma}\mid X_{t}=\sigma)\cdot\mathbb{P}(X_{t}=\sigma)}{\mathbb{P}(X_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma})}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\mathbb{P}(X_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma}\mid X_{t}=\sigma)\cdot\mathbb{P}(X_{t}=\sigma)}{\mathbb{P}(X_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This defines a valid reverse kernel based on the forward transition probabilities and the marginal distributions <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the expression used in <a href="#S3.Ex2" title="In Reverse Process ‣ 3 Discrete Diffusion through Conditionals ‣ Discrete Diffusion with Sample-Efficient Estimators for Conditionals" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. However, other reverse processes may exist that yield the same marginals.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Y_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Y_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be another sequence of random variables over <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(Y_{t}=\sigma)=\mathbb{P}(X_{t}=\sigma)=\mu_{t}(\sigma)\quad\text{for all }t\in\{0,\dots,T\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(Y_{t}=\sigma)=\mathbb{P}(X_{t}=\sigma)=\mu_{t}(\sigma)\quad\text{for all }t\in\{0,\dots,T\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a valid alternative reverse process if it satisfies the marginal constraints above.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As an illustrative example, consider the extreme case where the reverse kernel is marginally independent of the conditioning variable:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(Y_{t}=\sigma\mid Y_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma})=\mu_{t}(\sigma)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(Y_{t}=\sigma\mid Y_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma})=\mu_{t}(\sigma).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, the reverse step simply resamples from the marginal <math id="A3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, ignoring the previous state <math id="A3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Now fix <math id="A3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{T}=X_{T}\sim\mu_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{T}=X_{T}\sim\mu_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and define <math id="A3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> recursively using (2). Then we show by induction that:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(Y_{t}=\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}(Y_{t}=\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex55.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}\mathbb{P}(Y_{t}=\sigma\mid Y_{t+1}=\hat{\sigma})\cdot\mathbb{P}(Y_{t+1}=\hat{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}\mathbb{P}(Y_{t}=\sigma\mid Y_{t+1}=\hat{\sigma})\cdot\mathbb{P}(Y_{t+1}=\hat{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex56"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}\mu_{t}(\sigma)\cdot\mu_{t+1}(\hat{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}\mu_{t}(\sigma)\cdot\mu_{t+1}(\hat{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex57"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mu_{t}(\sigma)\cdot\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}\mu_{t+1}(\hat{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mu_{t}(\sigma)\cdot\sum_{\hat{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}\mu_{t+1}(\hat{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex58"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mu_{t}(\sigma)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mu_{t}(\sigma),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">since <math id="A3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a probability distribution and thus sums to 1.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In this degenerate case, the reverse kernel <math id="A3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\text{rev}}_{t}:\Sigma^{q}\times\Sigma^{q}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\text{rev}}_{t}:\Sigma^{q}\times\Sigma^{q}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by:</p>
<table id="A3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\text{rev}}_{t}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma}):=\mathbb{P}(Y_{t}=\sigma\mid Y_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma})=\mu_{t}(\sigma)\quad\forall\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\text{rev}}_{t}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma}):=\mathbb{P}(Y_{t}=\sigma\mid Y_{t+1}=\tilde{\sigma})=\mu_{t}(\sigma)\quad\forall\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">That is, <math id="A3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\text{rev}}_{t}(\cdot,\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\text{rev}}_{t}(\cdot,\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> is simply the marginal distribution <math id="A3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, regardless of the value of <math id="A3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>. This reverse kernel completely ignores the conditioning state and independently resamples <math id="A3.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\sim\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\sim\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at each step.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">While this kernel does not capture the time-reversal of the actual forward dynamics, it still guarantees the correct marginal distributions at every time step:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex59"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu_{t}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu_{t}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex59.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}k^{\text{rev}}_{t}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})\cdot\mu_{t+1}(\tilde{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}k^{\text{rev}}_{t}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})\cdot\mu_{t+1}(\tilde{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mu_{t}(\sigma)\cdot\sum_{\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}\mu_{t+1}(\tilde{\sigma})=\mu_{t}(\sigma)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mu_{t}(\sigma)\cdot\sum_{\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}}\mu_{t+1}(\tilde{\sigma})=\mu_{t}(\sigma).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">This construction shows that the reverse process is not uniquely determined by the marginal sequence <math id="A3.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mu_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mu_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and highlights a family of reverse dynamics that can be arbitrarily different from the canonical reverse Markov process.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, the set of all admissible reverse kernels that satisfy the marginal condition is convex; any convex combination of two valid reverse kernels <math id="A3.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\text{rev}}_{t,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\text{rev}}_{t,1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\text{rev}}_{t,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\text{rev}}_{t,2}</annotation></semantics></math> also yields a valid reverse kernel,</p>
<table id="A3.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\text{rev}}_{t}=\lambda k^{\text{rev}}_{t,1}+(1-\lambda)k^{\text{rev}}_{t,2},\quad\text{for any }\lambda\in[0,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>for any </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\text{rev}}_{t}=\lambda k^{\text{rev}}_{t,1}+(1-\lambda)k^{\text{rev}}_{t,2},\quad\text{for any }\lambda\in[0,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p12.2" class="ltx_p">This further underscores the flexibility and ambiguity inherent in defining reverse-time dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">It is important to emphasize that the transitions defined by general reverse kernels are <em id="A3.SS1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not local</em>. Unlike the canonical reverse process where transitions are typically constrained to move between configurations that differ by a single spin (i.e., Hamming distance one) this degenerate reverse kernel allows transitions between any two configurations in <math id="A3.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, regardless of their Hamming distance:</p>
<table id="A3.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\text{rev}}_{t}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})=\mu_{t}(\sigma)\quad\text{for all }\sigma,\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\text{rev}}_{t}(\sigma,\tilde{\sigma})=\mu_{t}(\sigma)\quad\text{for all }\sigma,\tilde{\sigma}\in\Sigma^{q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, starting from any configuration <math id="A3.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, the reverse process can jump to any other configuration <math id="A3.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> in a single step, with probability determined solely by the marginal <math id="A3.SS1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. There is no notion of continuity or neighborhood preserved by the dynamics. This contrasts sharply with reverse processes, where transitions are typically limited to configurations that differ by only one coordinate.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, while the degenerate reverse process is mathematically valid and correctly reproduces the marginal distributions <math id="A3.SS1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, it does not preserve the locality structure of the forward process. Its ability to transition freely between any two configurations in <math id="A3.SS1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> , without regard for neighborhood structure, leads to a reverse kernel that is inherently non-local. In high-dimensional spaces, such non-local kernels operate over the entire <math id="A3.SS1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{q}\times\Sigma^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{q}\times\Sigma^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> transition space, making them exponentially more complex to represent, learn, or approximate. On the other hand, kernels for local update rules scale linearly and generalize more easily.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Numerical Implementation Details</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Model Architecture</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the following architecture for each of the cases :</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Input block:
<math id="A4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Linear}(d_{\text{in}}\to h)\rightarrow\mathrm{LayerNorm}(h)\rightarrow\mathrm{SiLU}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Linear</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>LayerNorm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>SiLU</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Linear}(d_{\text{in}}\to h)\rightarrow\mathrm{LayerNorm}(h)\rightarrow\mathrm{SiLU}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="A4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math> is dependent on the denoising algorithm.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hidden blocks (up to <math id="A4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D{-}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D{-}1</annotation></semantics></math> blocks, depending on depth <math id="A4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\{1,\dots,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\{1,\dots,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>):
<math id="A4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Linear}(h\to h)\rightarrow\mathrm{LayerNorm}(h)\rightarrow\mathrm{SiLU}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Linear</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>LayerNorm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>SiLU</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Linear}(h\to h)\rightarrow\mathrm{LayerNorm}(h)\rightarrow\mathrm{SiLU}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Output layer:
<math id="A4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Linear}(h\to 2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Linear</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Linear}(h\to 2)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Shared hyperparameter sweep</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For every dataset–denoising algorithm combination, we run a small hyperparameter optimization loop over the following parameter using the <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hyperopt package</span> in Python:</p>
<ul id="A4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Depth:</span> <math id="A4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D\in\{1,..,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\{1,..,5\}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Width:</span> <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\{64,128,256,512\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>64,128,256,512</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\{64,128,256,512\}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Noise parameter</span> (Only for NeurISE Diffusion): <math id="A4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Noising time horizon (Only for NeurISE Diffusion): <math id="A4.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in[0,2,...10]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in[0,2,...10]</annotation></semantics></math> </span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning rate: <math id="A4.I2.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm lr}\in(10^{-4},5\times 10^{-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>lr</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm lr}\in(10^{-4},5\times 10^{-2})</annotation></semantics></math></span> with log uniform distribution.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Weight decay: <math id="A4.I2.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in(10^{-8},10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">𝐂𝐋𝐎𝐒𝐄</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in(10^{-8},10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math></span> with log uniform distribution.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Batch Size: <math id="A4.I2.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[64,128,256,512]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>64,128,256,512</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[64,128,256,512]</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>SEDD Implementation details</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the implementation of SEDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we introduced a final layer that enforced positivity in the output of the layer for the score approximation. While it is claimed that the loss function introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite> naturally forces the output of the network towards non-negativity, we did not observe this in our implementation, and in fact found that the training algorithm returned NaNs if the final layer was not appropriately augmented. Additionally, the inputs were required to be one-hot coded entirely in order for algorithm to show any significant learning. In contrast, for the Neurise Diffusion and D3PM only conditioning parameters were required to be one-hot coded.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
